/// Static behavioral API documentation for the backend.
/// The document is a compile-time literal and reads no actor state.
mixin () {
  /// Return the backend's public API documentation as Markdown.
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Backend API\n\nThis canister persists **named music compositions** for the offline music generator. Generation, playback and export all run in the browser; the backend only stores and returns the opaque JSON payload the frontend generator produces, so a saved composition survives a page reload.\n\n## Data model\n\nA composition is a record:\n\n- `id : Nat` — server-assigned, monotonically increasing identifier.\n- `name : Text` — display name, trimmed of leading/trailing spaces on save.\n- `payload : Text` — **opaque JSON text** produced by the frontend generator. The backend never parses or interprets its musical structure; it stores and returns the string verbatim.\n- `createdAt : Nat` — creation time in **nanoseconds** since the Unix epoch (`Time.now()`), as a `Nat`.\n- `updatedAt : Nat` — last-modified time in nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.\n\nThe `owner` field is stored internally but is **never returned** across the API boundary; the shared view (`CompositionView`) exposes only `id`, `name`, `payload`, `createdAt` and `updatedAt`.\n\n## Authentication and identity\n\nEvery composition method is caller-scoped. The caller's principal is taken from the message, never from an argument:\n\n- `listCompositions`, `getComposition` — `query` methods; they read the caller's principal and return only that caller's data.\n- `saveComposition`, `updateComposition`, `deleteComposition` — update methods; they write only under the caller's principal.\n\nThere is no separate sign-in gate on these methods: an anonymous caller is a valid principal and simply owns its own (empty) set of compositions. A caller only ever sees and mutates compositions whose stored `owner` equals its own principal.\n\nThe app's frontend pins an Internet Identity **derivation origin**, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent that already holds the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires. A principal derived against a different origin is a **different principal** than the one the frontend registered, so it sees a different set of compositions.\n\n## Methods\n\n### `listCompositions() : async [CompositionView]`\n\nReturns every composition owned by the caller, **newest first** (sorted by `createdAt` descending). Returns an empty array when the caller has none.\n\n### `getComposition(id : Nat) : async ?CompositionView`\n\nReturns the caller's composition with the given `id`, or `null` when no such composition exists **or** it belongs to another caller. The two cases are deliberately indistinguishable, so the method never reveals the existence of another caller's composition.\n\n### `saveComposition(name : Text, payload : Text) : async Result<CompositionView, CompositionError>`\n\nCreates a new composition owned by the caller and returns its view. The `id` is assigned by the server; `createdAt` and `updatedAt` are both set to the current time. The `name` is trimmed of leading/trailing spaces before storage.\n\n### `updateComposition(id : Nat, name : Text, payload : Text) : async Result<CompositionView, CompositionError>`\n\nReplaces the `name` and `payload` of the caller's composition with the given `id`. `createdAt` is preserved; `updatedAt` is set to the current time. The `name` is trimmed before storage.\n\n### `deleteComposition(id : Nat) : async Result<(), CompositionError>`\n\nPermanently removes the caller's composition with the given `id`. This is **destructive and not reversible**.\n\n## Errors\n\nMutations return `Result<_, CompositionError>` where `CompositionError` is:\n\n- `#invalidName` — the `name` is empty after trimming whitespace.\n- `#notFound(id)` — no composition with that `id` exists.\n- `#notAuthorized` — the composition exists but is owned by another caller.\n\n`getComposition` does not use this variant type: it returns `null` for both \"missing\" and \"not yours\".\n\n## Units and encodings\n\n- Timestamps (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`) are **nanoseconds** since the Unix epoch, typed `Nat`. Divide by 1_000_000_000 for seconds.\n- `payload` is a `Text` string containing JSON. It is stored and returned byte-for-byte; the backend applies no validation, normalization or size interpretation to it.\n- `id` values are `Nat` and increase monotonically; they are never reused after a delete.\n\n## Lifecycle, polling and retry safety\n\n- All methods are single-message calls with no asynchronous work and no background jobs. There is nothing to poll: a call's result is final when it returns.\n- `listCompositions` and `getComposition` are `query` calls and read committed state; a read immediately after a successful write reflects that write.\n- **Retry safety:** `saveComposition` is **not idempotent** — retrying it after an ambiguous failure creates a second composition with a new `id`. To retry safely, call `listCompositions` first and check whether the composition was already created. `updateComposition` and `deleteComposition` are idempotent in effect: re-running `updateComposition` with the same arguments yields the same stored state (only `updatedAt` advances), and re-running `deleteComposition` after a successful delete returns `#notFound(id)` rather than corrupting anything.\n\n## Non-obvious gotchas\n\n- The `owner` field is internal; clients cannot read or set it. Ownership is always the caller's principal.\n- `payload` is opaque JSON text. The backend does not validate it, so a client must ensure it is well-formed before saving if it intends to reload it.\n- `getComposition` returning `null` does not distinguish \"does not exist\" from \"belongs to someone else\".\n- `name` is trimmed on both save and update; a name consisting only of spaces is rejected with `#invalidName`.\n- Composition data is per-principal. Switching Internet Identity accounts (or deriving against a different origin) shows a different, separate set of compositions.\n";
  };
};
